export default {"version":1,"built":"2026-10-23T07:29:46.632Z","embed_model":"text-embedding-3-small","dims":512,"docs":{"s1":{"title":"The Economic Impacts of Jeju 4·3 Dark Tourism","type":"society_article","trust":"secondary","language":"en","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/Articles/article-economic.pdf","author":"Taewon Kim","date":"2026-05-15"},"s2":{"title":"Labelling Theory and the Construction of \"Communist\" Identities: The Case of the Jeju 4.3 Incident","type":"society_article","trust":"secondary","language":"en","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/Articles/article-labelling.pdf","author":"Jiwoong Moon","date":"2026-04-23"},"s3":{"title":"Long-Term Economic Scarring from Mass Violence: The Legacy of the Jeju 4.3 Incident","type":"society_article","trust":"secondary","language":"en","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/Articles/article-scarring.pdf","author":"Curie Kim","date":"2026-04-23"},"s4":{"title":"The Ethics of Remembering and Forgetting","type":"society_article","trust":"secondary","language":"en","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/Articles/article-ethics.pdf","author":"Kyudo Kim","date":"2026-04-23"},"s5":{"title":"Intergenerational Trauma and Psychological Repression in Survivors","type":"society_article","trust":"secondary","language":"en","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/Articles/article-trauma.pdf","author":"Minjae Kang","date":"2026-04-23"},"s6":{"title":"A Legal Analysis of the Jeju 4.3 Incident","type":"society_article","trust":"secondary","language":"en","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/Articles/article-legal.pdf","author":"Tyga Lee","date":"2026-06-15"},"about_en":{"title":"About Camellia Keepers","type":"site_page","trust":"secondary","language":"en","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/","author":"Camellia Keepers","date":null},"about_ko":{"title":"카멜리아 키퍼스 소개","type":"site_page","trust":"secondary","language":"ko","url":"https://camelliakeepers.com/","author":"Camellia Keepers","date":null}},"chunks":[{"d":"s1","p":1,"t":"The Economic Impacts of Jeju 4·3 Dark Tourism While Jeju Island is internationally recognized for its natural attractions—including its volcanic landscape, citrus production, and traditional haenyeo diving communities—it also encompasses significant historical sites associated with the 1948–1954 Jeju 4·3 Incident. In recent years, a growing cohort of tourists has traveled to Jeju specifically to visit these memorial sites and gain historical understanding, a phenomenon commonly referred to as \"4·3 dark tourism.\" This form of heritage tourism has emerged as a meaningful component of Jeju's economic landscape. This article examines the nature of the 4·3 Incident, defines 4·3 dark tourism within the broader context of memorial tourism, and analyzes its quantifiable economic impacts on local businesses, employment, and regional revenue generation. Jeju 4·3 Incident The Jeju 4·3 Incident, named for its commencement date of April 3, 1948, represents one of the most significant episodes of civil unrest and military repression in twentieth-century Korean history."},{"d":"s1","p":1,"t":"Jeju 4·3 Incident The Jeju 4·3 Incident, named for its commencement date of April 3, 1948, represents one of the most significant episodes of civil unrest and military repression in twentieth-century Korean history. During the politically unstable transition period between the conclusion of Japanese colonial administration and the establishment of separate Korean states, the island experienced a leftist-led uprising that was met with an extensive military and security force response, involving both South Korean government forces and American military oversight. The conflict persisted over a period of approximately six years. Contemporary historical estimates indicate that approximately 30,000 inhabitants of Jeju—representing approximately one in nine island residents—were killed during this period, with entire villages subject to systematic destruction (Jeju Tourism Organization). A distinctive feature of the 4·3 Incident in Korean historical memory is the prolonged official denial and suppression that followed the immediate conflict period."},{"d":"s1","p":1,"t":"A distinctive feature of the 4·3 Incident in Korean historical memory is the prolonged official denial and suppression that followed the immediate conflict period. For nearly five decades, public discussion and acknowledgment of the incident remained politically constrained. Survivors and bereaved family members experienced what historians have characterized as a \"double trauma,\" encompassing both the direct grief of loss and the institutional prohibition against commemorative expression. The establishment of the 4·3 Special Law in 2000 initiated the process of official state recognition. President Roh Moo-hyun became the first sitting president to issue a formal governmental apology in 2003, followed by President Moon Jae-in's public acknowledgment at the seventieth anniversary commemoration in 2018 (Jeju 4·3 Peace Foundation). 4·3 Dark Tourism Dark tourism, in its broadest conceptualization, encompasses travel to destinations historically associated with death, suffering, or catastrophic events."},{"d":"s1","p":1,"t":"4·3 Dark Tourism Dark tourism, in its broadest conceptualization, encompasses travel to destinations historically associated with death, suffering, or catastrophic events. Exemplary sites include Auschwitz, the Cambodian genocide memorials, the 9/11 Memorial, and the Hiroshima Peace Memorial. The term was formally introduced by Lennon and Foley in the mid-1990s and has subsequently developed into a recognized and substantial sector of the global travel industry."},{"d":"s1","p":2,"t":"Market analysis indicates that the global dark tourism sector was valued at approximately USD 32.6 billion in 2024, with projections suggesting expansion to approximately USD 57 billion by 2032 (Credence Research). Within the context of Jeju Island, 4·3 dark tourism assumes a distinctive form centered on specific memorial and educational institutions. The primary institutional anchor is the Jeju 4·3 Peace Park, which commenced operations in 2008 following extended processes of legal establishment and political negotiation. Occupying an area of approximately 400,000 square meters in the Bonggae-dong district, the park comprises a memorial hall, a chronologically organized museum documenting the historical events preceding and following the uprising, an enshrinement facility containing memorial tablets for 14,000 identified victims, and a \"Tombstone Park for the Missing,\" which contains approximately 4,000 markers commemorating individuals whose remains were not recovered (Jeju 4·3 Peace Foundation)."},{"d":"s1","p":2,"t":"Complementary to the Peace Park are guided heritage tours operated by organizations including Jeju Dark Tours, an organization founded in 2017 by human rights advocate Baek Gayoon. These guided experiences direct visitors to specific historical sites within the broader geographic region, including Bukchon village, where substantial casualties occurred in a single-day operation; Darangshi Cave, where the remains of eleven villagers were discovered in 1992; Seodal Oreum, an execution site in the southwestern portion of the island; and Moksimul Cave, which served as temporary refuge for civilian populations during military operations. Tours are typically conducted in Korean with English-language interpretation available upon request, operate on varying timescales from single-day to multi-night programs, and range in cost from approximately ₩50,000 to ₩250,000 per participant."},{"d":"s1","p":2,"t":"Tours are typically conducted in Korean with English-language interpretation available upon request, operate on varying timescales from single-day to multi-night programs, and range in cost from approximately ₩50,000 to ₩250,000 per participant. The primary distinction between such heritage tourism and conventional recreational sightseeing lies in the prioritization of historical education and interpretive comprehension as the primary objective of the visitor experience. Direct Economic Impact Although the Jeju 4·3 Peace Park operates on a free admission basis, the facility has demonstrated substantial annual visitor traffic. Estimated annual visitation has increased progressively since the early 2010s, expanding from approximately 320,000 visitors in 2015 to an estimated 720,000 in 2024, with the exception of a substantial decline during 2020–2021 corresponding to the COVID-19 pandemic. Significant increases in visitation occurred during 2018 and 2023, coinciding with the seventieth and seventy-fifth anniversary commemoration periods (see Figure 1)."},{"d":"s1","p":2,"t":"Significant increases in visitation occurred during 2018 and 2023, coinciding with the seventieth and seventy-fifth anniversary commemoration periods (see Figure 1). The annual April 3 memorial ceremony attracts approximately 10,000 participants, and the memorial museum has administered educational programs for tens of thousands of school-based groups annually (Jeju 4·3 Peace Foundation)."},{"d":"s1","p":3,"t":"These temporal variations in visitation demonstrate correlation with periods of increased media coverage and public commemorative activity. Both identified anniversary years coincided with substantial media attention across television, print, and digital platforms, together with associated increases in social media engagement. The documented relationship between commemorative events and increased visitation numbers indicates the capacity of historical memory initiatives to drive sustained cultural tourism. However, the primary mechanism of economic impact operates independently of direct admission revenue, which approximates zero. Rather, economic benefit derives from visitor expenditures ancillary to the visitation experience. According to survey research conducted by the Jeju Research Institute in 2023, visitors to 4·3 heritage sites maintained an average length of stay of 2.7 days on Jeju Island and expended an estimated ₩285,000 per person across the categories of accommodation, food service, transportation, and commodity purchases (Jeju Research Institute)."},{"d":"s1","p":3,"t":"According to survey research conducted by the Jeju Research Institute in 2023, visitors to 4·3 heritage sites maintained an average length of stay of 2.7 days on Jeju Island and expended an estimated ₩285,000 per person across the categories of accommodation, food service, transportation, and commodity purchases (Jeju Research Institute). Extrapolating from the estimated annual visitation of 700,000 individuals engaged with 4·3-related sites yields an aggregate estimate of approximately ₩199.5 billion (approximately USD 145 million) in annual ancillary expenditure circulating through Jeju's regional economy. Indirect Impact on Local Businesses The geographic distribution of 4·3 memorial sites across the island produce economic consequences distinct from those associated with conventional beach-and-resort tourism models. Rather than concentrating visitor expenditure in centralized resort areas such as Jungmun and Aewol, dark tourism visitation generates distributed spending patterns across smaller communities and peripheral economic zones."},{"d":"s1","p":3,"t":"Rather than concentrating visitor expenditure in centralized resort areas such as Jungmun and Aewol, dark tourism visitation generates distributed spending patterns across smaller communities and peripheral economic zones. This dispersal pattern has resulted in measurable"},{"d":"s1","p":4,"t":"benefits for food service establishments in proximity to the Peace Park, particularly within the Bonggae-dong and Jocheon districts. Research conducted by the Jeju Chamber of Commerce in 2022 examined the economic performance of small-scale food service establishments operating within a three-kilometer radius of the Peace Park facility. The findings indicated that such establishments experienced an average revenue increase of 38 percent during peak heritage tourism seasons—specifically April through June and September through November—compared to the same establishments' performance a decade earlier (Jeju Chamber of Commerce). This increase reflects the integration of local culinary traditions into guided tour itineraries, as authorized guides are incentivized to direct visitor expenditure toward independently operated establishments rather than multinational food service chains. Regional specialties including local pork barbecue, abalone-based soups, and traditional Jeju noodle preparations feature prominently in tour programming."},{"d":"s1","p":4,"t":"Regional specialties including local pork barbecue, abalone-based soups, and traditional Jeju noodle preparations feature prominently in tour programming. The accommodation sector in Bukchon village provides a particularly illustrative example of dark tourism's economic impact on historically economically marginal communities. Bukchon village, the site of significant casualties during a single military operation, experienced prolonged economic stagnation in the post-conflict period. Contemporary economic revitalization of the area has been achieved in part through the development of heritage tourism infrastructure, including restored traditional structures adapted for visitor accommodation. These establishments maintain occupancy rates substantially elevated during the April commemorative period, with accommodation pricing often doubling during this annually scheduled heritage tourism peak."},{"d":"s1","p":5,"t":"Figure 2 presents the estimated distribution of per-visitor expenditure of ₩285,000. Accommodation accounts for approximately 32 percent of visitor spending, food and beverage service for 28 percent, ground transportation for 14 percent, guided tour services for 12 percent, local commodity purchases for 9 percent, and miscellaneous expenditures including donations and entry fees to privately operated heritage sites for the remaining 5 percent. The concentration of expenditure on guided tour services—approximately 12 percent of total visitor spending—represents a substantively elevated proportion relative to mainstream tourism patterns on Jeju, which typically generate guide service fees representing less than 4 percent of total visitor expenditure. This differential reflects the interpretive and educational requirements inherent in heritage tourism experiences, wherein the historical and contextual significance of physical locations necessitates professional explanation. Secondary economic benefits extend to the publishing and educational product sectors."},{"d":"s1","p":5,"t":"Secondary economic benefits extend to the publishing and educational product sectors. Independent bookstores operating on Jeju maintain inventory of translated editions of literary works addressing the 4·3 Incident, including Hyun Ki-young's novel Sun-i Samch'on (Aunt Suni), a foundational literary treatment that initiated significant public discourse regarding previously suppressed historical events. Educational publishers document sustained demand from school-based groups conducting curricular visits to the memorial complex. Small-scale printing operations located in Jeju City maintain specialization in the production of tour informational materials, exhibition catalogs, and commemorative publications supporting the broader 4·3 heritage tourism infrastructure. Employment and Longer-Term Effects Quantitative assessment of employment generation attributable to 4·3 heritage tourism presents methodological challenges due to the diffuse nature of economic integration across multiple employment sectors. However, several baseline observations can be substantiated."},{"d":"s1","p":5,"t":"However, several baseline observations can be substantiated. The Jeju 4·3 Peace Park operates with a permanent staff of approximately 80 individuals, supplemented by seasonal docents and security personnel. Organizations including Jeju Dark Tours and comparable heritage tourism operators maintain between two and ten full-time personnel, together with supplementary freelance guide services. Extrapolating employment effects across the broader tourism infrastructure supporting 4·3 visitation—encompassing accommodations, food service, transportation, and retail sectors—conservative estimates place the aggregate employment base at approximately 1,200 to 1,800 Jeju residents whose income streams depend substantially on heritage tourism activity. Contemporary research literature on dark tourism and memorial tourism identifies a phenomenon designated as the \"memorial dividend,\" characterized by sustained patterns of visitor traffic demonstrating greater recession resistance relative to conventional leisure-based tourism models (Sharpley and Stone)."},{"d":"s1","p":5,"t":"Contemporary research literature on dark tourism and memorial tourism identifies a phenomenon designated as the \"memorial dividend,\" characterized by sustained patterns of visitor traffic demonstrating greater recession resistance relative to conventional leisure-based tourism models (Sharpley and Stone). Auschwitz-Birkenau and the Hiroshima Peace Memorial, both operating at the international scale, continue to demonstrate sustained visitation levels more than seven decades following the historical events they commemorate. Comparable trajectories appear probable for Jeju 4·3 heritage tourism, particularly given demographic trends including"},{"d":"s1","p":6,"t":"increased international dissemination of Korean-language educational content and growing cohorts of both Korean and international visitors pursuing historically informed travel. Concurrent with these economic considerations exist substantive ethical and social questions regarding the appropriate commemoration and interpretation of historical trauma. Portions of the survivor and bereaved family communities have articulated concerns regarding the potential commodification of collective tragedy, particularly when visitor conduct at memorial sites manifests insufficient respect for the gravity of historical events. Optimal management of these memorial spaces therefore necessitates integration of heritage tourism development with protocols ensuring historical accuracy, visitor comportment standards, and preservation of the dignity of affected communities—considerations extending beyond purely economic assessment frameworks."},{"d":"s1","p":6,"t":"Optimal management of these memorial spaces therefore necessitates integration of heritage tourism development with protocols ensuring historical accuracy, visitor comportment standards, and preservation of the dignity of affected communities—considerations extending beyond purely economic assessment frameworks. Conclusion While 4·3 dark tourism does not function as a dominant driver of regional tourism economics relative to conventional resort-based leisure tourism on Jeju, it operates as a significant and strategically important economic sector within the island's broader tourism portfolio. The phenomenon directs visitor expenditure into economically peripheral communities and sectors historically underrepresented in mainstream tourism development, including small-scale accommodation providers, independent food service establishments, and local transportation services."},{"d":"s1","p":6,"t":"The phenomenon directs visitor expenditure into economically peripheral communities and sectors historically underrepresented in mainstream tourism development, including small-scale accommodation providers, independent food service establishments, and local transportation services. The quantifiable economic impacts—estimated at approximately ₩199.5 billion in annual visitor expenditure—are complemented by qualitative contributions to cultural preservation, historical education, and community economic revitalization. The significance of 4·3 dark tourism extends beyond purely economic metrics. For survivors and bereaved communities, the development of heritage tourism infrastructure represents formal governmental and institutional recognition of suppressed historical events. The sustained flow of both domestic and international visitors pursuing historical understanding constitutes a mechanism through which collective memory operates and historical consciousness develops."},{"d":"s1","p":6,"t":"The sustained flow of both domestic and international visitors pursuing historical understanding constitutes a mechanism through which collective memory operates and historical consciousness develops. The economic sustainability of memorial sites and heritage tourism services is fundamentally interconnected with the capacity to attract and serve visitors motivated by educational and commemorative purposes. Sustainable management frameworks for such sites must therefore integrate economic viability with historical accuracy, ethical representation, and the dignity of affected populations—ensuring that heritage tourism functions as a vector for historical understanding rather than commodification of tragedy."},{"d":"s1","p":7,"t":"Bibliography Cameron Thacker, Todd. \"A History of April 3rd, Victims' Stories, and How to Learn from the Tragedy.\" Jeju Dark Tours, www.jejudarktours.org/en/tour-review/a-history-of-april-3rd-victims-stories-and-how-to-l earn-from-the-tragedy/. Accessed 27 May 2026. Credence Research. Dark Tourism Market Size, Growth, Share and Forecast 2032. Credence Research, 2025, www.credenceresearch.com/report/dark-tourism-market. Jeju Chamber of Commerce. Annual Report on Small Business Performance in the Bonggae-Jocheon Region. Jeju Chamber of Commerce, 2022. Jeju 4·3 Peace Foundation. \"Jeju 4·3 Peace Park: Memorial Site.\" From Truth to Peace, jeju43peace.org/jeju-4-3-peace-park/jeju-4-3-peace-park-_-memorial-site/. Accessed 27 May 2026. Jeju Research Institute. Visitor Spending Patterns at Heritage Sites in Jeju Special Self-Governing Province. Jeju Research Institute, 2023. Jeju Tourism Organization. \"A Painful History: Examining Jeju's April 3rd Incident.\" Jeju Tourism Organization Travel Blog, 3 Apr."},{"d":"s1","p":7,"t":"\"A Painful History: Examining Jeju's April 3rd Incident.\" Jeju Tourism Organization Travel Blog, 3 Apr. 2017, jejutourism.wordpress.com/2017/04/03/a-painful-history-examining-jejus-april-3rd-incid ent/. Lennon, John, and Malcolm Foley. Dark Tourism: The Attraction of Death and Disaster. Continuum, 2000. Sharpley, Richard, and Philip R. Stone, editors. The Darker Side of Travel: The Theory and Practice of Dark Tourism. Channel View Publications, 2009."},{"d":"s2","p":1,"t":"Labelling Theory and the Construction of “Communist” Identities: The Case of the Jeju 4.3 Incident The Jeju 4.3 Incident (1947–1954) remains one of the most controversial and traumatic events in modern Korean history. While it began as a complex uprising rooted in political, social, and economic grievances, it was subsequently framed by the South Korean state as a “communist rebellion.” This labelling had profound consequences: it justified state violence, shaped public perception, and erased the identities of thousands of victims. Using labelling theory, this essay argues that the construction of “communist” identities during the Jeju 4.3 Incident was not simply descriptive but actively produced a social reality that enabled mass violence and long-term suppression of truth. Labelling Theory: A Framework Labelling theory, developed in sociology, suggests that deviance is not inherent in an act but is socially constructed through labels imposed by authorities."},{"d":"s2","p":1,"t":"Labelling Theory: A Framework Labelling theory, developed in sociology, suggests that deviance is not inherent in an act but is socially constructed through labels imposed by authorities. Once individuals or groups are labelled (e.g., “criminal” or “communist”), this label shapes how others perceive them and how institutions treat them. Importantly, labels can become self-fulfilling and justify unequal or violent treatment. In the context of Jeju 4.3, the label “communist” functioned as a powerful political tool. It transformed a diverse population—many of whom were civilians—into perceived enemies of the state. Historical Context and the Emergence of Labels"},{"d":"s2","p":2,"t":"Following the end of Japanese colonial rule in 1945, Korea was divided along ideological lines during the early Cold War. Jeju Island became a site of tension, with widespread dissatisfaction toward the U.S. military government and upcoming elections. While leftist groups were indeed active, the situation was far more complex than a simple ideological uprising. However, the South Korean government and allied forces framed the entire event as a “communist insurgency.” This narrative reduced the complexity of local grievances into a single ideological category. According to historical research, tens of thousands of people were killed during the suppression, with government forces responsible for the majority of civilian deaths. This shows that the label “communist” was applied broadly, often without distinction between armed rebels and ordinary civilians."},{"d":"s2","p":3,"t":"Institutionalisation of Labels and Everyday Control Another critical aspect of labelling in the Jeju 4.3 Incident is how the “communist” identity became embedded within formal institutions and everyday governance, transforming it from a temporary political accusation into a long-term system of control. Rather than remaining a rhetorical tool, the label was operationalised through administrative mechanisms such as police records, residency documentation, and ideological screening processes. Under the influence of the broader Cold War climate, South Korean authorities expanded surveillance practices that categorised citizens based on perceived political loyalty. This meant that labelling extended beyond those directly involved in resistance activities to include family members, neighbours, and even entire villages. In line with labelling theory, this reflects how deviance can be constructed and maintained through institutional reinforcement, where the state not only defines who is “deviant” but also systematically reproduces that identity across"},{"d":"s2","p":4,"t":"generations. For many Jeju residents, being associated with the label “communist” resulted in restricted access to education, employment, and social mobility, effectively embedding inequality into the structure of society. Schools, workplaces, and local administrations became sites where these labels were quietly enforced, often without formal acknowledgement but with significant consequences. Furthermore, the bureaucratisation of labelling contributed to its persistence, as records and classifications were rarely challenged or revised, even decades after the initial events. This created a form of “archival stigma,” where individuals remained marked by past accusations regardless of changing political conditions. At the same time, the normalisation of such systems led to widespread self-censorship, as individuals adapted their behaviour to avoid suspicion. This demonstrates how labelling operates not only through overt violence but also through subtle, everyday practices that regulate behaviour and maintain social order."},{"d":"s2","p":4,"t":"This demonstrates how labelling operates not only through overt violence but also through subtle, everyday practices that regulate behaviour and maintain social order. By institutionalising the “communist” label, the state ensured that its effects would endure far beyond the immediate period of conflict, shaping identities, opportunities, and social relations in ways that were both pervasive and difficult to dismantle. Graph: Estimated Responsibility for Deaths This data highlights a critical contradiction: while the state labelled victims as “communists,” it was primarily state forces that carried out the killings. The label, therefore,e functioned less as an accurate description and more as a justification for repression. The Power of the “Communist” Label"},{"d":"s2","p":5,"t":"The label “communist” carried extreme weight in the Cold War context. It implied disloyalty, danger, and subversion. By applying this label to Jeju residents, the state achieved several objectives: 1. Dehumanization Victims were no longer seen as civilians but as enemies. This made violence against them more acceptable. The treatment of bodies—such as disposal or concealment—further reflects this dehumanisation and the attempt to erase identities. 2. Legitimisation of Violence Labelling the uprising as communist justified military suppression. It framed violence as necessary for national security rather than as human rights violations. 3. Silencing and Fear For decades, discussing the incident was prohibited. Victims’ families remained silent out of fear of being associated with “communism.” The massacre was hidden from public knowledge for nearly 50 years. Evidence of Labelling in Media and Memory The persistence of labellinis is evident in media representations. Until the late 1980s, newspapers consistently described the event as a “communist riot,” reinforcing the state narrative."},{"d":"s2","p":5,"t":"Until the late 1980s, newspapers consistently described the event as a “communist riot,” reinforcing the state narrative. Timeline of Media Narratives This shift demonstrates how labels are not fixed but can change over time as political contexts evolve. However, the earlier label had already caused lasting damage by shaping collective memory. Social Consequences of Labelling The labelling of Jeju residents as “communists” had long-term social consequences:"},{"d":"s2","p":6,"t":"1. Stigmatisation of Survivors Families of victims were marginalised and discriminated against. Being associated with the incident could limit education and employment opportunities. 2. Collective Silence and Trauma The fear of being labelled prevented open discussion, leading to what scholars describe as intergenerational trauma. The silence itself became a continuation of violence. 3. Distortion of Historical Truth By controlling the narrative, the state shaped how history was understood. The incident was not officially investigated until decades later. Re-evaluation and the Deconstruction of Labels With democratisation in South Korea after 1987, the dominant narrative began to change. A truth commission established in 2000 investigated the events and recognised the suffering of victims. This process represents an attempt to deconstruct the label of “communist” and restore the identities of those affected. Jeju has since been reframed as a symbol of peace and reconciliation rather than rebellion. However, the legacy of labelling persists."},{"d":"s2","p":6,"t":"However, the legacy of labelling persists. Competing interpretations—such as those emphasising the role of communist insurgents—continue to influence political debates today. Conclusion The Jeju 4.3 Incident demonstrates the powerful role of labelling in shaping historical events. Through the application of labelling theory, it becomes clear that the term “communist” was not merely descriptive but actively constructed a social reality. It justified violence, erased individual identities, and silenced survivors for decades. Ultimately, the re-evaluation of the incident in recent decades shows that labels can be challenged and redefined. However, the long-lasting effects of the original label highlight the dangers of allowing political narratives to define human identities. The case of Jeju 4.3 serves as a reminder that labels are not neutral—they carry the power to shape lives, histories, and collective memory. Bibliography 1. Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation (n.d.) The Jeju 4.3 Incident. Available at: https://www.jeju43peace.or.kr (Accessed: 23 April 2026). 2."},{"d":"s2","p":6,"t":"2. UNESCO (2023.) Revealing Truth: Jeju 4.3 Archives. Available at: https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/revealing-truth-jeju-43-archives (Accessed: 23 April 2026). 3. Seoul National University (n.d.) Research on the Jeju 4.3 Incident. Available at: https://s-space.snu.ac.kr (Accessed: 23 April 2026)."},{"d":"s2","p":7,"t":"4. Oxford University Press (2012) International Journal of Transitional Justice: Jeju 4.3 and Historical Memory. Available at: https://academic.oup.com (Accessed: 23 April 2026). 5. Korean Studies Information Service System (n.d.) Media Representation of the Jeju 4.3 Incident. Available at: https://www.kci.go.kr (Accessed: 23 April 2026). 6. Wikimedia Commons (n.d.) Jeju Uprising Image Archive. Available at: https://commons.wikimedia.org (Accessed: 23 April 2026)."},{"d":"s3","p":1,"t":"Long-Term Economic Scarring from Mass Violence: The Legacy of the Jeju 4.3 Incident The trajectory of a society’s economic development is often explained through measurable factors such as policy decisions, natural resources, technological progress, and participation in global trade. However, beneath these visible indicators lies a deeper and often overlooked force: the enduring damage caused by mass violence. Long-term economic scarring refers to the persistent and structural reduction in a society’s productive capacity following periods of intense conflict. Unlike cyclical economic downturns, which may recover over time, the effects of mass violence can permanently alter a region’s developmental path. This occurs through the destruction of human capital, the collapse of economic and social institutions, and the erosion of trust that underpins all economic interaction. The Jeju 4.3 Incident provides a powerful and deeply illustrative case study of this phenomenon."},{"d":"s3","p":1,"t":"The Jeju 4.3 Incident provides a powerful and deeply illustrative case study of this phenomenon. Between 1948 and 1954, the island of Jeju experienced a period of violent state suppression that resulted in the deaths of approximately 30,000 people—around 10% of its population—and the destruction of more than 70% of its villages. While the immediate human cost was devastating, the long-term economic consequences were equally profound. These effects became embedded in Jeju’s social and economic fabric, delaying its development for decades and widening the gap between the island and mainland South Korea. I. The Destruction of Human Capital At its core, an economy is sustained by human capital—the collective skills, knowledge, health, and labor capacity of its population. Mass violence represents one of the most severe forms of human capital destruction, as it not only eliminates individuals but also disrupts the systems through which knowledge and skills are passed on."},{"d":"s3","p":1,"t":"Mass violence represents one of the most severe forms of human capital destruction, as it not only eliminates individuals but also disrupts the systems through which knowledge and skills are passed on. During the Jeju 4.3 Incident, violence disproportionately targeted working-age adults, particularly men who formed the backbone of the island’s agricultural and fishing economy. This led to an immediate “labor vacuum,” sharply reducing productive capacity. Farms were left untended, fisheries underutilized, and local industries struggled to function even at a basic level."},{"d":"s3","p":2,"t":"As illustrated in Figure 1, the demographic impact was heavily concentrated among those in their prime working years. Individuals aged 10 to 39 accounted for over 66% of the total victims, intensifying the labor vacuum and stripping the island not only of its current workforce but also of its future economic leaders. However, the damage extended far beyond this immediate labor shortage. The incident also triggered a deeper and more enduring form of economic disruption: intergenerational knowledge loss. In 1948, Jeju’s economy was largely subsistence-based, relying on agricultural techniques adapted to its volcanic soil and unique climate. These methods were rarely written down; instead, they were transmitted through oral tradition and daily practice. The deaths of elders, village leaders, and skilled workers severed this chain of knowledge. As a result, survivors were left without the expertise and mentorship needed to sustain productivity, leading to long-term declines in agricultural efficiency. The demographic consequences further compounded these challenges."},{"d":"s3","p":2,"t":"The demographic consequences further compounded these challenges. The large-scale loss of working-age individuals skewed the population toward dependents—the elderly and orphaned children—creating a high dependency ratio. This forced the remaining workforce to focus on immediate survival rather than long-term investment in education, innovation, or capital development. Over time, this imbalance created a self-reinforcing cycle of low productivity and limited economic mobility, effectively stalling economic growth for decades. II. Physical Destruction and the \"Scorched Earth\" Policy The economic scarring of Jeju was not only demographic but also physical. The government’s “scorched earth” policy systematically dismantled the island’s infrastructure, leaving lasting damage that extended far beyond the years of active conflict. More than 300 villages were burned, erasing entire communities and the local economic networks that sustained them. These villages were not merely residential areas but centers of production, trade, and social organization."},{"d":"s3","p":2,"t":"These villages were not merely residential areas but centers of production, trade, and social organization. Their destruction disrupted the exchange of goods and services, effectively collapsing local markets and isolating surviving populations. Agriculture, the foundation of Jeju’s pre-incident economy, was particularly hard hit. Livestock were slaughtered or lost during the violence, irrigation systems fell into disrepair, and fields were abandoned. Because agriculture depends heavily on continuity—seasonal cycles, soil management, and accumulated experience—the interruption had long-lasting consequences. Even after the violence subsided, the absence of labor, tools, and knowledge meant that recovery was slow and incomplete. In the immediate aftermath, Jeju struggled to produce enough food to sustain its own population. One of the most significant long-term consequences was the creation of “red zones”—areas designated as unsafe or politically suspect, which remained abandoned for decades."},{"d":"s3","p":2,"t":"One of the most significant long-term consequences was the creation of “red zones”—areas designated as unsafe or politically suspect, which remained abandoned for decades. These lands, once productive, were effectively removed from economic use for over 50 years, representing a massive loss of potential output. This physical devastation contributed directly to regional inequality. While mainland South Korea embarked on rapid industrialization during the 1960s—often referred to as the “Miracle on the Han River”—Jeju remained focused on basic reconstruction. The island lacked the infrastructure, labor force, and capital necessary to participate in this period of accelerated growth. As a result, a clear development gap emerged, with Jeju becoming economically peripheral and structurally disadvantaged compared to the mainland."},{"d":"s3","p":3,"t":"Economic Sector Pre-Incident Status Post-Incident Scarring Agriculture Self-sufficient; traditional techniques 70% infrastructure loss; decades of low yields Labor Robust working-age population 10% total population loss; massive \"brain drain\" Land Use Managed village commons \"Red Zones\" (abandoned) for 50+ years III. The Erosion of Social Trust and Institutional Neglect While the destruction of human and physical capital is visible, the erosion of social trust is a more subtle yet equally damaging consequence of mass violence. Economic activity depends fundamentally on trust—the expectation that individuals can safely engage in trade, investment, and cooperation without fear of exploitation or persecution. In the aftermath of the Jeju 4.3 Incident, this trust was deeply undermined. The South Korean government labeled Jeju as a “Red Island,” associating its მოსახლtion with political subversion. Under the system of guilt by association (Yeon-jwa-je), the families and descendants of victims were subjected to discrimination, surveillance, and exclusion from public sector employment."},{"d":"s3","p":3,"t":"Under the system of guilt by association (Yeon-jwa-je), the families and descendants of victims were subjected to discrimination, surveillance, and exclusion from public sector employment. This created what can be described as a “silence tax.” Individuals avoided drawing attention to themselves, refrained from participating in collective activities, and hesitated to pursue entrepreneurial ventures. The risk of being labeled politically suspect outweighed the potential benefits of economic initiative. This pervasive fear suppressed innovation, reduced investment, and weakened the social networks necessary for economic cooperation. Institutional neglect further exacerbated these issues. Because the history of the incident was politically suppressed, Jeju did not receive adequate government investment in infrastructure, education, or public services. Schools were underfunded, transportation networks underdeveloped, and healthcare systems insufficient."},{"d":"s3","p":3,"t":"Schools were underfunded, transportation networks underdeveloped, and healthcare systems insufficient. This lack of institutional support reinforced the island’s economic stagnation, creating a cycle in which underdevelopment justified further neglect. Over time, these factors combined to produce a deeply entrenched economic disadvantage. Unlike regions that benefited from state-led industrial policies, Jeju remained marginalized, its development constrained not only by material losses but also by the absence of trust and institutional support. IV. The Turning Point: Reconciliation as Economic Stimulus"},{"d":"s3","p":4,"t":"The case of Jeju demonstrates that economic recovery from mass violence is inseparable from social and political reconciliation. It was only in the late 20th and early 21st centuries, following South Korea’s democratization, that meaningful efforts were made to address the legacy of the 4.3 Incident. A key turning point came with the official state apology in 2003, which acknowledged the government’s role in the violence. This act of recognition played a crucial role in restoring dignity to survivors and their descendants. By removing political stigmas and dismantling discriminatory systems, it allowed Jeju residents to participate more fully in the national economy. This shift also enabled a transformation in Jeju’s economic strategy. The island began to reposition itself as a global tourism destination, leveraging its natural beauty and unique cultural heritage. Importantly, sites associated with the 4.3 Incident were not erased but recontextualized."},{"d":"s3","p":4,"t":"Importantly, sites associated with the 4.3 Incident were not erased but recontextualized. The Jeju 4.3 Peace Park stands as a symbol of this transformation, turning a place of historical trauma into a site of education, remembrance, and cultural engagement. Tourism brought significant investment, infrastructure development, and job creation, allowing Jeju to integrate more fully into both national and global markets. However, this transformation was delayed by decades due to the unresolved legacy of the incident. The case thus highlights how unresolved historical trauma can act as a barrier to economic development, while reconciliation can serve as a powerful catalyst for growth. Conclusion The Jeju 4.3 Incident illustrates that the consequences of mass violence extend far beyond the immediate loss of life. The long-term economic scars—manifested in the destruction of human capital, the dismantling of infrastructure, and the erosion of social trust—created a “lost half-century” for Jeju Island."},{"d":"s3","p":4,"t":"The long-term economic scars—manifested in the destruction of human capital, the dismantling of infrastructure, and the erosion of social trust—created a “lost half-century” for Jeju Island. These effects were not temporary setbacks but structural changes that altered the island’s developmental trajectory. Recovery required more than physical reconstruction. It depended on restoring trust, addressing historical injustices, and reintegrating marginalized communities into the broader economic system. The eventual success of Jeju’s tourism industry demonstrates that healing and development are deeply interconnected. Ultimately, the case of Jeju offers a broader lesson for policymakers and societies worldwide. The prevention of mass violence and the protection of human dignity are not only moral imperatives but also essential foundations for sustainable economic development. Once inflicted, the scars of violence are costly and enduring, shaping the fate of communities for generations. Bibliography ● Jeju 4·3 Peace Foundation (n.d.) Fact and Truth of the Jeju 4·3 Incident."},{"d":"s3","p":4,"t":"Bibliography ● Jeju 4·3 Peace Foundation (n.d.) Fact and Truth of the Jeju 4·3 Incident. Available at: http://jeju43peace.org/historytruth/fact-truth/ (Accessed: 23 April 2026). ● Han, S. J. (2012) The Jeju 4.3 Incident and the Struggle for Justice in South Korea. London: Routledge."},{"d":"s3","p":5,"t":"● Jeju 4·3 Peace Foundation (n.d.) Jeju 4·3 Official Website. Available at: http://www.jeju43.go.kr (Accessed: 23 April 2026)."},{"d":"s4","p":1,"t":"The Ethics of Remembering vs. Forgetting: Camelia Keepers — Abstract This essay examines the philosophical and political tensions between collective remembering and institutionalised forgetting, using the Jeju April Third Incident (4.3 사건) as its primary case study. Situated within the broader discourse of transitional justice and memory ethics, the essay argues that deliberate amnesia imposed by state power constitutes not merely a historiographical failure but an ethical transgression against the dead, the surviving, and the civic community. Drawing upon scholarship from Paul Ricoeur, Aleida Assmann, and Henry Rousso, alongside empirical data from the Jeju 4.3 Research Institute and official government investigations, the essay contends that the belated acknowledgment of the Jeju massacre illuminates the moral stakes inherent in all politics of memory. I."},{"d":"s4","p":1,"t":"I. Introduction: The Moral Architecture of Memory In his monumental work Memory, History, Forgetting (2004), Paul Ricoeur distinguished between two fundamentally different modalities of forgetting: the forgetting that is wrought by the erasure of traces, and the forgetting that is “reserved”—a latent preservation kept at the threshold of recollection, awaiting the conditions of its return. The Jeju April Third Incident (4·3 사건) belongs emphatically to the first category. What unfolded on the island of Jeju between April 3, 1948, and September 21, 1954, was not merely suppressed: it was actively unmade, its victims unnamed, its perpetrators shielded by the architecture of Cold War ideology and an authoritarian state’s self-serving historiography. That this silence persisted for nearly half a century renders the ethical question of memory not merely academic but urgently practical. The tension between remembering and forgetting is not, of course, unique to Korea."},{"d":"s4","p":1,"t":"The tension between remembering and forgetting is not, of course, unique to Korea. From the Armenian Genocide to the Rwandan massacres, from the desaparecidos of Argentina to the victims of South Africa’s apartheid state, the question of how societies reckon with mass atrocity is among the most contested domains of both ethics and politics. Yet Jeju occupies a singular place in this"},{"d":"s4","p":2,"t":"global reckoning. It is a case in which the transition from silence to acknowledgment was extraordinarily slow—stretching across five decades of democratic and semi-democratic governance—and yet ultimately, if imperfectly, achieved. The Jeju 4.3 Special Act of 2000 and the subsequent governmental apology by President Roh Moo-hyun in 2003 represent landmark moments in the global history of transitional justice. They also provide an unusually well-documented record of what is lost, and what may be partially recovered, when collective memory is first extinguished and then, haltingly, rekindled. II. The Historical Contours of the Incident To understand the ethics of forgetting in Jeju’s case, one must first reckon with the magnitude of what was forgotten. The April Third Incident was precipitated by the uprising of the South Korean Workers’ Party (SKWP) on Jeju Island against the U.S. Military Government and the nascent south Korean state, which the insurgents viewed as an illegitimate anti-communist client regime."},{"d":"s4","p":2,"t":"Military Government and the nascent south Korean state, which the insurgents viewed as an illegitimate anti-communist client regime. What began as a politically motivated insurrection was met with a counter-insurgency operation of overwhelming, disproportionate brutality. According to the 2003 findings of the Jeju 4.3 Incident Investigation Report—a document produced by the government-commissioned Truth Commission following the Special Act—between 14,000 and 30,000 people perished during the seven-year period of violence, with most estimates converging around 25,000 to 30,000 fatalities (Jeju 4.3 Research Institute, 2003). Given Jeju’s population of approximately 300,000 at the time, this represents a death toll constituting roughly one-tenth of the entire island population. Entire mountain villages were razed. Over 70% of the island’s villages were either burned or destroyed (Merrill, 1989). Approximately 39,285 homes were reported destroyed and an estimated 230 villages were annihilated (Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation, 2014)."},{"d":"s4","p":2,"t":"Approximately 39,285 homes were reported destroyed and an estimated 230 villages were annihilated (Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation, 2014). The commission’s report identified the south Korean state security forces and the Northwest Youth Corps (Seobuk Cheongnyeon-dan)—a paramilitary organisation of refugees from North Korea—as the primary perpetrators of civilian massacres, though it also documented killings carried out by the insurgents themselves. Crucially, the state’s own culpability was unambiguously acknowledged: “The government bears a great responsibility for the massacre of innocent people in the name of anti-communist operations” (Jeju 4.3 Incident Investigation Report, 2003, p. 536). This acknowledgement, belated as it was, is without precedent in South Korean political history and marks an epistemological rupture of considerable ethical significance."},{"d":"s4","p":3,"t":"Figure 1: Scale of the Jeju 4.3 Incident (1948–1954). Source: Jeju 4.3 Incident Investigation Report (2003); Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation (2014). III. The Mechanics of Institutionalised Forgetting The suppression of Jeju’s memory was not accidental. It was systematic, deliberate, and enforced through law, ideology, and terror. Under the successive authoritarian governments of Syngman Rhee and then Park Chung-hee, any public discussion of the events of April Third was classified as proto-communist agitation. Survivors who spoke of the massacres were subject to surveillance, arrest, and in some documented cases, execution. The National Security Law, enacted in December 1948 in the direct aftermath of the uprising, provided the juridical infrastructure for this suppression, criminalising speech and assembly deemed sympathetic to communism with penalties extending to capital punishment (Kim, 2000)."},{"d":"s4","p":3,"t":"The National Security Law, enacted in December 1948 in the direct aftermath of the uprising, provided the juridical infrastructure for this suppression, criminalising speech and assembly deemed sympathetic to communism with penalties extending to capital punishment (Kim, 2000). The French historian Henry Rousso, writing of the Vichy syndrome in France—another case of prolonged collective denial—coined the concept of “amnesia as strategy” to describe the deliberate political instrumentalisation of forgetting (Rousso, 1991). In Jeju’s case, this strategic amnesia was particularly pronounced: survivors were compelled not merely to remain silent but to actively participate in the falsification of memory, lest they or their descendants be branded as communist sympathisers. The second-generation testimonies collected by the Jeju 4.3 Research Institute reveal a pervasive culture of intergenerational silence, in which grandparents refused to tell grandchildren the"},{"d":"s4","p":4,"t":"fates of those who had disappeared, not out of indifference but out of a protective terror (Jeju 4.3 Research Institute, 2008). This compelled silence is, ethically speaking, a form of secondary victimisation. Scholars of trauma studies have long established that the denial of narrative—the inability to bear witness, to name the dead, to speak the experience—constitutes a form of ongoing harm that may, in clinical terms, be as debilitating as the original trauma itself (Herman, 1992). When the state enforces this silence, the ethical weight of the forgetting is correspondingly intensified. It is no longer merely an absence of memory but an act of violence committed against the very capacity for memory. Figure 2: Timeline from Enforced Silence to Official Acknowledgement (1948–2021). Sources: Jeju 4.3 Incident Investigation Report (2003); Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation (2021). IV."},{"d":"s4","p":4,"t":"IV. The Ethics of Memory: Philosophical Foundations The philosophical literature on memory ethics has grown substantially since the 1990s, informed in large part by the proliferation of truth commissions, memorialisation projects, and demands for official apology across the post-Cold War world. Among the most influential frameworks is that of Aleida Assmann, whose distinction between “storage memory” (Speichergedächtnis) and “functional memory” (Funktionsgedächtnis) has proven particularly productive (Assmann, 2008). Storage memory is the archive: the passive repository of all that is preserved but not actively utilised in collective identity formation. Functional memory, by contrast, is the selective, narratively organised subset of the archive that a community actively employs in the construction of its self-understanding."},{"d":"s4","p":5,"t":"What makes the Jeju case ethically instructive is that the suppression of 4.3 operated at both levels simultaneously. The state not only prevented the archive from being formed—destroying documents, suppressing testimonies, forbidding scholarship—but also actively colonised functional memory by substituting an official narrative that cast the victims as communists deserving of their fate. This double operation is what distinguishes mere neglect from what the philosopher Margaret Urban Walker (2010) calls “moral repair” failure: the refusal to acknowledge wrongdoing in ways that would permit the wronged party’s moral standing to be restored. Ricoeur’s concept of “just memory” (mémoire juste) is equally pertinent here."},{"d":"s4","p":5,"t":"Ricoeur’s concept of “just memory” (mémoire juste) is equally pertinent here. Writing against both the excesses of traumatic over-remembering (which he associated with the kind of identity politics that militarises memory) and the pathology of forgetting, Ricoeur argued for a form of memory that is oriented by the obligation of justice: one that remembers specifically in order to acknowledge the claims of the victim upon the living (Ricoeur, 2004, pp. 86–92). On this account, the ethical imperative to remember Jeju is not merely sentimental or historiographical: it is a demand grounded in the basic moral requirement to treat persons—including the dead—as ends in themselves rather than as instruments of political narrative. V."},{"d":"s4","p":5,"t":"V. The Politics of Acknowledgement: Transitional Justice and Its Limits The passage of the Jeju 4.3 Special Act in January 2000, followed by the publication of the official government investigation report in 2003, and President Roh Moo-hyun’s formal apology on April 3, 2003—the fifty-fifth anniversary of the uprising—represented a tectonic shift in South Korea’s politics of memory. The apology was, by any comparative standard, remarkable: a sitting head of state assuming institutional responsibility for atrocities committed by preceding governments. In this, South Korea joined a small cohort of states—including Germany, Argentina, and South Africa—that have sought formal reconciliation with the victims of state violence through official acknowledgement rather than continued erasure. And yet the process has been neither linear nor uncontested. As recently as 2019, conservative political forces within South Korea sought to revise the 4.3 narrative, reintroducing language that framed the uprising primarily as a communist insurrection rather than as a civilian massacre."},{"d":"s4","p":5,"t":"As recently as 2019, conservative political forces within South Korea sought to revise the 4.3 narrative, reintroducing language that framed the uprising primarily as a communist insurrection rather than as a civilian massacre. Such revisionism illustrates a pattern observed across multiple post-conflict societies: that the politics of memory is never settled but remains permanently vulnerable to the re-instrumentalisation of the past by competing political interests. According to survey data collected by the Jeju 4.3 Peace"},{"d":"s4","p":6,"t":"Foundation in 2021, approximately 34.6% of South Koreans aged 18–29 reported having “little or no knowledge” of the Jeju incident, suggesting that even after two decades of official memorialisation, the transmission of historical consciousness across generations remains fragile (Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation, 2021). This fragility is not incidental. It reflects a structural tension within all transitional justice processes between what Naomi Roht-Arriaza and Javier Mariezcurrena (2006) have called “the justice cascade”—the expanding global norm of accountability for mass atrocity—and the persistent counter-pressure of national political interests that resist full reckoning. The Jeju 4.3 Peace Park, opened in 2008, and the annual April Third memorial ceremony, designated a national commemorative day in 2014, represent institutional achievements of considerable significance. Yet the memorialisation infrastructure remains politically contingent: its funding, its curriculum integration in schools, and its official framing have all been subject to revision under successive administrations."},{"d":"s4","p":6,"t":"Yet the memorialisation infrastructure remains politically contingent: its funding, its curriculum integration in schools, and its official framing have all been subject to revision under successive administrations. Figure 3: Public Awareness of the Jeju 4.3 Incident by Age Group, South Korea (2021). Source: Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation Survey (2021). VI. Against the Pragmatics of Forgetting: A Defence of Memory"},{"d":"s4","p":7,"t":"It would be intellectually dishonest not to acknowledge that there exists a serious philosophical tradition that counsels, under certain circumstances, a pragmatics of forgetting. Ernest Renan’s famous observation in “What Is a Nation?” (1882) that “every French citizen has to have forgotten the Night of Saint Bartholomew, or the massacres in the Midi in the thirteenth century” articulates the view that national cohesion may require the selective suppression of traumatic memory. More recently, scholars such as David Rieff (2016) have argued that “in praise of forgetting,” certain historical grievances, if perpetually rehearsed, fuel cycles of resentment and violence rather than contributing to reconciliation. These arguments, while not without merit in specific contexts, do not apply to the Jeju case—nor, it should be argued, to cases of state-perpetrated mass violence against civilian populations more generally. The pragmatics of forgetting presupposes that memory is already present and may be voluntarily relinquished in the service of a broader good."},{"d":"s4","p":7,"t":"The pragmatics of forgetting presupposes that memory is already present and may be voluntarily relinquished in the service of a broader good. In Jeju’s case, memory was never freely given. It was violently taken. To advocate for forgetting in such a context is not to counsel prudent restraint but to ratify the original crime. As the political philosopher Avishai Margalit argued in The Ethics of Memory (2002), while individuals may choose to forgive and even forget personal wrongs, the community bears a special obligation to maintain what he terms “shared memory” of moral atrocities, precisely because such atrocities are directed not merely against individuals but against humanity’s shared moral order. Moreover, the empirical evidence from comparative transitional justice studies suggests that societies which undertake meaningful processes of truth-telling and acknowledgement are, over time, more rather than less stable than those which do not."},{"d":"s4","p":7,"t":"Moreover, the empirical evidence from comparative transitional justice studies suggests that societies which undertake meaningful processes of truth-telling and acknowledgement are, over time, more rather than less stable than those which do not. A comparative analysis of twenty post-conflict societies by Tricia Olsen, Leigh Payne, and Andrew Reiter (2010) found that truth commissions combined with prosecutions produced the most robust improvements in human rights outcomes, while amnesty without acknowledgement correlated with higher rates of subsequent political violence. Memory, on this evidence, is not merely an ethical obligation to the dead: it is a precondition for the sustainable peace of the living."},{"d":"s4","p":8,"t":"Figure 4: Transitional Justice Mechanisms and Human Rights Outcomes across 20 Post-Conflict Societies. Source: Olsen, Payne & Reiter (2010). VII. Conclusion: The Unfinished Work of Memory The Jeju April Third Incident is, in the end, a story about the fragility and the necessity of truth. Approximately 14,000 to 30,000 people were killed in a period of seven years. For nearly half a century, their deaths were officially denied, their names erased from public record, their surviving families subjected to a regime of enforced silence that was itself a form of ongoing violence. The eventual acknowledgement—partial, contested, politically vulnerable—does not undo the harm. It cannot. The dead remain dead. The testimonies lost to a generation of fearful silence are irretrievable. What acknowledgement can do is something more modest but no less important: it can restore to the victims their moral standing as persons wronged, rather than enemies justly defeated. It can allow the living to grieve without shame."},{"d":"s4","p":8,"t":"It can allow the living to grieve without shame. And it can, in Ricoeur’s formulation, provide the foundation for a “just memory” oriented not by vengeance but by the recognition of shared humanity across the boundary of death. The ethics of remembering, as illuminated by Jeju, is not the ethics of obsession or of grievance perpetually renewed. It is the ethics of witness: the refusal to allow the state’s violence to complete itself by also erasing the record of its commission. In a world in which such violence continues—in which states still massacre civilians and then mobilise the apparatus of official forgetting to protect"},{"d":"s4","p":9,"t":"themselves from accountability—the lesson of Jeju is not merely historical. It is a standing obligation, renewed each April Third, to insist that what happened shall not be unmade. Bibliography Assmann, A. (2008). “Transforms between History and Memory.” Social Research, 75(1), 49–72. Herman, J. L. (1992). Trauma and Recovery: The Aftermath of Violence. Basic Books. Jeju 4.3 Incident Investigation Report. (2003). National Commission for the Investigation of the Truth about the Jeju 4.3 Incident. Republic of Korea Government. Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation. (2014). Jeju 4.3 Historical Data Collection. Jeju Special Self-Governing Province. Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation. (2021). Survey on Public Awareness of the Jeju 4.3 Incident. Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation Research Series. Jeju 4.3 Research Institute. (2003). Report on the Jeju April Third Incident. Jeju 4.3 Research Institute Press. Jeju 4.3 Research Institute. (2008). Testimonies of the Second Generation: Oral History Project. Jeju 4.3 Research Institute. Kim, D. C. (2000)."},{"d":"s4","p":9,"t":"(2000). The National Security Law and the Politics of Anti-Communism in South Korea. Seoul National University Press. Margalit, A. (2002). The Ethics of Memory. Harvard University Press. Merrill, J. (1989). Korea: The Peninsular Origins of the War. University of Delaware Press. Olsen, T., Payne, L., & Reiter, A. (2010). Transitional Justice in Balance: Comparing Processes, Weighing Efficacy. United States Institute of Peace Press. Renan, E. (1882). “Qu’est-ce qu’une nation?” Sorbonne lecture. Translated in Bhabha, H. (Ed.), Nation and Narration (1990). Routledge. Ricoeur, P. (2004). Memory, History, Forgetting. Trans. K. Blamey & D. Pellauer. University of Chicago Press. Rieff, D. (2016). In Praise of Forgetting: Historical Memory and Its Ironies. Yale University Press. Roht-Arriaza, N., & Mariezcurrena, J. (Eds.) (2006). Transitional Justice in the Twenty-First Century: Beyond Truth versus Justice. Cambridge University Press. Rousso, H. (1991). The Vichy Syndrome: History and Memory in France Since 1944. Trans. A. Goldhammer. Harvard University Press. Walker, M. U. (2010)."},{"d":"s4","p":9,"t":"(2010). What Is Reparative Justice? Marquette University Press."},{"d":"s5","p":1,"t":"Intergenerational Trauma and Psychological Repression in Survivors Introduction The Jeju 4.3 Incident stands as one of the most traumatic and long-silenced events in modern Korean history. Beginning on April 3, 1948, and continuing in waves of violence until 1954, the incident resulted in the deaths of an estimated 25,000 to 30,000 people—approximately 10% of Jeju Island’s population at the time. What makes this event particularly significant is not only the scale of violence but the enduring psychological consequences that extended far beyond the immediate victims. Survivors were not merely left to cope with grief; they were forced into silence by a political climate that suppressed memory itself. As a result, the Jeju 4.3 Incident offers a powerful case study of how trauma can be both internalized (psychological repression) and transmitted across generations (intergenerational trauma). Historical Context To understand the psychological aftermath, it is necessary to first examine the historical conditions that produced it."},{"d":"s5","p":1,"t":"Historical Context To understand the psychological aftermath, it is necessary to first examine the historical conditions that produced it. Following Korea’s liberation from Japanese colonial rule in 1945, ideological tensions between left-wing and right-wing groups intensified under the influence of Cold War geopolitics. On Jeju Island, resistance against the planned separate elections in the South—viewed by many as cementing division—sparked protests that escalated into armed conflict. The South Korean government, supported by U.S. military authorities, responded with severe counterinsurgency measures. Entire villages were burned, and civilians were indiscriminately labeled as communist sympathizers. What followed was not only mass violence but systematic erasure. For decades, the South Korean state framed the incident as a communist rebellion, effectively criminalizing survivors and the bereaved. Public discussion was suppressed, and even mentioning the incident could lead to suspicion or punishment."},{"d":"s5","p":1,"t":"Public discussion was suppressed, and even mentioning the incident could lead to suspicion or punishment. This prolonged silence created the conditions for psychological repression, where individuals suppress traumatic memories not by choice, but as a survival mechanism within an oppressive social structure. Photo of Jeju 4.3 Incident Distribution of deaths during Jeju 4.3 Incident"},{"d":"s5","p":2,"t":"Psychological Repression in Survivors Psychological repression, as understood in trauma theory, refers to the unconscious exclusion of distressing memories from conscious awareness. In the context of Jeju 4.3, repression functioned both individually and collectively. Survivors often avoided speaking about their experiences, even within their own families. This was not simply denial—it was a form of protection. Speaking out could result in social ostracization, loss of employment, or even state surveillance. However, repression does not eliminate trauma; it merely buries it. Many survivors exhibited symptoms now associated with post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), including nightmares, hypervigilance, and emotional numbing. Yet, due to the stigma surrounding the event and the lack of psychological support at the time, these symptoms remained largely unacknowledged. One of the most striking aspects of this repression is its normalization. Silence became embedded in daily life, shaping communication patterns and emotional expression."},{"d":"s5","p":2,"t":"Silence became embedded in daily life, shaping communication patterns and emotional expression. Parents avoided discussing the past with their children, creating a generational gap not only in knowledge but in emotional understanding. The absence of narrative became a defining feature of the trauma itself. Intergenerational Trauma Despite this silence, the trauma of Jeju 4.3 did not end with the survivors. It was transmitted—subtly yet powerfully—to subsequent generations. This phenomenon, known as intergenerational trauma, occurs when the psychological effects of a traumatic event are passed down to children and even grandchildren. In Jeju, this transmission often occurred through indirect means: emotional atmospheres, behavioral patterns, and fragmented stories. Children grew up sensing fear, anxiety, or unresolved grief in their parents, even if the cause was never explicitly explained. This aligns with psychological research suggesting that trauma can be communicated through nonverbal cues, such as tone, silence, and emotional withdrawal."},{"d":"s5","p":2,"t":"This aligns with psychological research suggesting that trauma can be communicated through nonverbal cues, such as tone, silence, and emotional withdrawal. Moreover, the social stigma attached to the incident reinforced this transmission. Families associated with victims were sometimes labeled as “red” (communist), affecting their social and economic opportunities. This created a persistent sense of insecurity and marginalization that extended beyond the original event. In this way, trauma became not only psychological but also structural, embedded in social identity and collective memory. Interestingly, the second and third generations often experienced a form of “postmemory”—a term coined by scholar Marianne Hirsch to describe the relationship that later generations have with the traumatic experiences of their predecessors. These individuals inherit memories they did not directly experience, yet feel deeply connected to them. In Jeju, many"},{"d":"s5","p":3,"t":"descendants have reported a strong emotional response upon learning about the incident later in life, often accompanied by a sense of anger, grief, or a desire for justice. Aftermath of the survivors The late 20th and early 21st centuries marked a turning point in the public acknowledgment of the Jeju 4.3 Incident. Democratization in South Korea created space for historical re-evaluation. In 2000, the government passed the Special Act on the Jeju 4.3 Incident, leading to an official investigation and the establishment of the Jeju 4.3 Peace Foundation. In 2003, President Roh Moo-hyun issued a formal apology on behalf of the state. These efforts represented more than political gestures; they were crucial steps toward psychological healing. Acknowledgment validates the experiences of survivors, allowing them to integrate their memories into a coherent narrative rather than suppressing them. Memorials, such as the Jeju 4.3 Peace Park, provide physical spaces where memory can be collectively processed. However, healing is neither immediate nor complete."},{"d":"s5","p":3,"t":"However, healing is neither immediate nor complete. For many survivors, decades of repression have left deep psychological scars. Some have only begun to speak about their experiences late in life, often expressing regret for the years of silence. For younger generations, the challenge lies in reconciling inherited trauma with contemporary identity—understanding the past without being defined by it. Personal Reflection What makes the Jeju 4.3 Incident particularly compelling is not just its historical significance, but its emotional resonance in the present. Growing up in Korea—or even learning about this event from afar—one cannot ignore the profound silence that once surrounded it. This silence is not empty; it is filled with suppressed voices, fragmented memories, and unspoken grief. Reflecting on this, one realizes that trauma is not only about what is remembered, but also about what is forgotten—or forced to be forgotten. The absence of stories can be as powerful as their presence."},{"d":"s5","p":3,"t":"The absence of stories can be as powerful as their presence. In many ways, the intergenerational impact of Jeju 4.3 challenges the assumption that time alone heals wounds. Without acknowledgment and dialogue, trauma can persist in invisible yet pervasive ways. At the same time, there is a sense of cautious hope. The increasing openness surrounding the incident suggests a shift from repression to recognition. Younger generations are engaging with the past through education, art, and public discourse. This does not erase the trauma, but it transforms it—from a source of silent suffering into a catalyst for understanding and empathy. Conclusion The Jeju 4.3 Incident illustrates how trauma operates across both time and silence. Survivors, constrained by political repression, internalized their suffering, leading to long-term psychological consequences. Yet, this trauma did not remain confined to one"},{"d":"s5","p":4,"t":"generation; it was transmitted through emotional, social, and cultural channels to their descendants. Understanding this process is essential not only for historical accuracy but for ethical responsibility. Recognizing intergenerational trauma allows societies to address the lingering effects of past violence, moving beyond denial toward meaningful reconciliation. In the case of Jeju, the gradual breaking of silence represents a critical step in this process. Ultimately, the legacy of Jeju 4.3 is not only one of tragedy, but also of resilience. It reminds us that while trauma can be inherited, so too can the capacity for healing—provided that memory is acknowledged, voices are heard, and silence is no longer enforced."},{"d":"s5","p":4,"t":"It reminds us that while trauma can be inherited, so too can the capacity for healing—provided that memory is acknowledged, voices are heard, and silence is no longer enforced. Bibliography https://www.pacifica.edu/dissertation-oral-defense/jeju-shamanism-healing-intergenerationaltrauma-through-collective-mourning/ https://transitionaljusticedata.org/public_files/reportTCID213.pdf https://www.npr.org/2022/09/07/1121427407/survivors-of-a-massacre-in-south-korea-are-still -seeking-an-apology-from-the-u-s https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/badfe97d-18d0-462d-8c7 1-556be4be618c/content"},{"d":"s6","p":1,"t":"A Legal Analysis of the Jeju 4.3 Incident I. Introduction The Jeju April Third Incident (1948 to 1954), in which South Korean military forces and right-wing auxiliaries killed an estimated 14,000 to 30,000 civilians, is one of the most legally significant episodes of state violence in modern Korean history. This essay argues that the incident constitutes grave violations of both domestic constitutional guarantees and emerging international human rights norms, that the South Korean legal system compounded these wrongs by actively prosecuting survivors under politically motivated legislation, and that while the Special Act of 2000 and its subsequent amendments represent a landmark in transitional justice, the framework remains legally incomplete without individual criminal accountability. II. The Military Tribunals: Due Process Denied The most direct legal atrocity within the 4.3 Incident was the operation of wartime military tribunals that sentenced thousands of Jeju civilians to death or imprisonment through proceedings that bore no resemblance to lawful adjudication."},{"d":"s6","p":1,"t":"The Military Tribunals: Due Process Denied The most direct legal atrocity within the 4.3 Incident was the operation of wartime military tribunals that sentenced thousands of Jeju civilians to death or imprisonment through proceedings that bore no resemblance to lawful adjudication. Following the declaration of martial law in November 1948, military courts processed detainees en masse, in many instances without individual hearings, legal representation, or meaningful opportunity to contest the charges. Confessions were extracted under torture, and the evidentiary record in most cases consisted of nothing more than a name on a list supplied by right-wing youth auxiliaries such as the Northwest Youth Association (서북청년회). The Jeju 4.3 Committee, established under the Special Act of 2000, documented in its 2003 Final Report that a significant number of the convictions imposed by these tribunals were factually baseless and procedurally void from inception."},{"d":"s6","p":1,"t":"The Jeju 4.3 Committee, established under the Special Act of 2000, documented in its 2003 Final Report that a significant number of the convictions imposed by these tribunals were factually baseless and procedurally void from inception. The Committee's findings constitute an official governmental determination that the tribunals violated the right to a fair trial, a right protected under Article 27 of the Constitution of the Republic of Korea and, from 1966 onward, under Articles 9 and 14 of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, to which South Korea acceded in 1990. The legal significance of this determination is substantial: it means that the state itself has acknowledged that thousands of convictions were unlawful, yet those convictions were not formally quashed by a court of law until the passage of the 2021 amendment to the Special Act, which enabled victims or their families to apply for judicial retrial. Even then, uptake has been limited and the process remains burdensome for elderly survivors. III."},{"d":"s6","p":1,"t":"III. The National Security Act and the Criminalization of Memory Beyond the tribunal period, the National Security Act (국가보안법, 1948) functioned for over five decades as a legal instrument that transformed victims into criminals. Its broad prohibition on activities deemed to benefit anti-state organizations was applied to suppress any public discussion of the 4.3 Incident under successive authoritarian governments, from Syngman Rhee through to the military governments of the 1970s and 1980s. Survivors who gave testimony, journalists who reported on the massacres, and academics who researched the events faced prosecution or forced self-censorship. The novelist Hyun Ki-young, whose 1978 short story Aunt Suni (순이 삼촌) brought the massacre to national literary attention,"},{"d":"s6","p":2,"t":"was arrested under the National Security Act and subjected to interrogation by the Korean Central Intelligence Agency, a case that became emblematic of how the law was weaponized against cultural memory. Legally, this pattern represents what scholars of transitional justice identify as structural impunity, that is the use of formally valid legislation to prevent accountability, suppress evidence, and protect perpetrators. The UN Human Rights Committee, in its 1999 Concluding Observations on South Korea, specifically identified the National Security Act's overbroad provisions as incompatible with the right to freedom of expression and opinion under Article 19 of the ICCPR, a direct international legal rebuke of the very instrument used to silence 4.3 survivors. The Act has yet to be repealed, and its continued existence on the statute books remains a point of legal and political contention. IV."},{"d":"s6","p":2,"t":"IV. The Special Act (2000) and the Unfinished Legal Reckoning The enactment of the Special Act on the Jeju April Third Incident in January 2000 marked a turning point, as for the first time the National Assembly formally acknowledged state responsibility and created a legal mechanism, the Jeju 4.3 Committee, to investigate facts, identify victims, restore honor, and recommend remedial measures. The Committee's 2003 Final Report, running to over 700 pages, constitutes the most comprehensive official legal record of the incident and formed the basis for President Roh Moo-hyun's April 2003 state apology, the first by a sitting Korean president. Under the doctrine of state responsibility as reflected in the International Law Commission's Articles on State Responsibility (2001), this apology carries legal weight as an official acknowledgment of internationally wrongful acts. The 2021 amendment further strengthened the framework by providing direct monetary compensation and enabling judicial retrials for survivors and bereaved families."},{"d":"s6","p":2,"t":"The 2021 amendment further strengthened the framework by providing direct monetary compensation and enabling judicial retrials for survivors and bereaved families. However, the Special Act framework contains a structural legal gap that no amendment has yet closed, in that it provides no mechanism for criminal prosecution of the perpetrators. Not a single military officer, government official, or paramilitary member has faced criminal charges for the killings. The Jeju District Court, in proceedings related to the 2019 retrial applications filed by survivors' families, rehabilitated victims administratively but declined to rule on individual criminal liability, citing the deaths of most perpetrators and the absence of a prosecutorial mandate in the Special Act."},{"d":"s6","p":2,"t":"The Jeju District Court, in proceedings related to the 2019 retrial applications filed by survivors' families, rehabilitated victims administratively but declined to rule on individual criminal liability, citing the deaths of most perpetrators and the absence of a prosecutorial mandate in the Special Act. This gap is legally significant because, under the evolving norm reflected in the 4.3 Committee's own report, the killings of unarmed civilians under military orders constituted unlawful homicide under the domestic criminal law in force at the time, meaning prosecution was always legally available, and its absence was a political choice rather than a legal impossibility. V. Conclusion The legal history of Jeju 4.3 teaches that state violence and legal failure are not parallel phenomena, as they are often the same event. From the void military tribunals of 1948 to the prosecutorial use of the National Security Act against survivors, law was the mechanism of oppression before it became, tentatively, the mechanism of remedy."},{"d":"s6","p":2,"t":"From the void military tribunals of 1948 to the prosecutorial use of the National Security Act against survivors, law was the mechanism of oppression before it became, tentatively, the mechanism of remedy. The Special Act and the 2021 amendments represent genuine and hard-won progress, but compensation and administrative rehabilitation are not substitutes for criminal accountability. The retrial proceedings at the Jeju District Court have restored individual honor, yet they have not delivered justice for the act of killing. Until South Korean law formally prosecutes or at least formally finds the perpetrators criminally liable in absentia, the legal response to 4.3 will remain a reparation without a verdict. That distinction matters, because law's authority rests not only on what it grants, but on what it is willing to call wrong."},{"d":"s6","p":3,"t":"Bibliography Cumings, Bruce. The Origins of the Korean War, Volume II: The Roaring of the Cataract, 1947 to 1950. Princeton University Press, 1990. Feffer, John. \"Jeju Island and the Legacy of April 3.\" Foreign Policy in Focus, April 3, 2008. Hyun, Ki-young. Aunt Suni (순이 삼촌). Chang Jak Kwa Bi Pyung Sa, 1978. Translated by Jennifer Lee, Columbia University Press, 2021. Jeju 4.3 Research Institute. The Jeju April Third Incident and Its Aftermath: A Documentary Record. Jeju Special Self-Governing Province, 2008. Kim, Hun Joon. The Massacres at Mt. Halla: Sixty Years of Truth Seeking in South Korea. Cornell University Press, 2014. National Committee for Investigation of the Truth about the Jeju April Third Incident. The Final Report of the Jeju 4.3 Committee. Republic of Korea, 2003. Republic of Korea, National Assembly. Special Act on Finding the Truth about the Jeju April Third Incident and Restoring the Honor of Victims, Act No. 6117. January 12, 2000, amended 2021. Roh, Moo-hyun. Presidential Apology for the Jeju April Third Incident."},{"d":"s6","p":3,"t":"Presidential Apology for the Jeju April Third Incident. Jeju Special Self-Governing Province, Republic of Korea, October 31, 2003. United Nations Human Rights Committee. Concluding Observations on the Republic of Korea, CCPR/C/79/Add.114. November 1, 1999. Yang, Joong-hae. \"Transitional Justice and the Jeju April Third Incident: Evaluating South Korea's Legislative Response.\" Journal of Korean Law, vol. 16, no. 1, 2016, pp. 1 to 38."},{"d":"about_en","p":null,"t":"OUR PURPOSE Remembranceis our responsibility. Camellia Keepers is a community of students and residents of Jeju Island, committed to the proper commemoration and recognition of the April 3rd Incident — a chapter of Korean history that shaped an island, silenced thousands, and demands that we never forget. 1948–1954 DURATION OF THE INCIDENT 25–35k ESTIMATED VICTIMS 76+ YEARS SINCE APRIL 3RD 2025 MIDDLE SCHOOL CURRICULUM YEAR 4·3 Who We Are AboutCamellia Keepers A community of students and residents of Jeju, committed to ensuring the April 3rd Incident is properly learned, remembered, and never reduced to a footnote. I. The Camellia of Jeju The dongbaek — camellia japonica — blooms through the coldest months of the Jeju winter. Unlike most flowers, its petals do not scatter one by one; the entire bloom falls at once, intact, from the branch. It is this quality that gave the camellia its meaning after the April 3rd Incident. The sudden, whole falling of the flower became a symbol for the thousands of lives cut short without warning during the massacres of 1948 to 1954."},{"d":"about_en","p":null,"t":"The sudden, whole falling of the flower became a symbol for the thousands of lives cut short without warning during the massacres of 1948 to 1954. Today, the red camellia badge is worn at every annual memorial on Jeju, and the flower serves as the official emblem of the April 3rd Incident. Camellia Keepers takes its name from this symbol — and from the responsibility to preserve the memory it carries. The camellia falls whole — so did they. The red camellia is worn each April 3rd by those who gather at the Jeju 4·3 Peace Park to remember the victims. The flower has become inseparable from the act of memory itself. II. The April 3rd Incident The Jeju 4·3 Incident presents a tragic history of dehumanisation, persecution, and oppression — born from the political fractures of a nation divided against its will."},{"d":"about_en","p":null,"t":"The April 3rd Incident The Jeju 4·3 Incident presents a tragic history of dehumanisation, persecution, and oppression — born from the political fractures of a nation divided against its will. Jeju Island, 1948–1954 — the years of the Incident 1945 — Division of Korea At the close of the Second World War, Korea faced an unprecedented crisis when the peninsula was divided into North and South along the 38th parallel — the North under Soviet occupation, the South under the United States. The prospect of permanent division created deep and irreconcilable tensions across the population. 1948 — A Separate Election Right-wing parties of the South declared an independent general election on May 10th, 1948, under approval of the United Nations Temporary Commission on Korea (UNTOK). Many feared this election would permanently end any hope of reunification. On Jeju Island, these tensions were especially acute. April 3rd, 1948 — The Uprising In opposition to the planned election, left-wing groups in Jeju rose against police stations and right-wing officials on April 3rd, 1948."},{"d":"about_en","p":null,"t":"April 3rd, 1948 — The Uprising In opposition to the planned election, left-wing groups in Jeju rose against police stations and right-wing officials on April 3rd, 1948. What began as a political protest evolved into a prolonged, state-led counterinsurgency campaign that descended into large-scale civilian massacre. 1948–1954 — The Massacres The conflict lasted six years. Between 25,000 and 35,000 people — a staggering proportion of Jeju's population — were killed in violent suppression campaigns. Villages were burned, families destroyed, and survivors subjected to lifelong trauma. The full scale of the atrocity was deliberately concealed for decades. Between 25,000 and 35,000 people — a significant fraction of Jeju's entire population — were killed in state-led suppression campaigns between 1948 and 1954. III. From Silence to Memory For decades after the massacres, information about the April 3rd Incident was suppressed. Survivors and their families were silenced. It was only in recent years that the full scope of what occurred became widely accessible and acknowledged."},{"d":"about_en","p":null,"t":"It was only in recent years that the full scope of what occurred became widely accessible and acknowledged. Today, few people in Korea are entirely unaware of the incident — yet awareness has not corrected widespread misconceptions. A considerable proportion still hold inaccurate understandings unless they have conducted independent research. Secondary school curricula only began addressing 4·3 in the early 2010s; it was added to the middle school curriculum in 2025. IV. Our Responsibility As residents and students studying on Jeju Island, we believe it is crucial to face the responsibility of properly learning and commemorating the 4·3 Incident. This is our main objective. While it may be impossible to fully convey the damage inflicted upon the victims, we believe it is equally valuable to raise awareness and expose the case to a wider audience. Camellia Keepers aims to bolster the recognition and correct perception of the April 3rd Incident — through articles, community, and an unwavering commitment to memory. V."},{"d":"about_en","p":null,"t":"V. The Board Camellia Keepers is led by students and residents of Jeju Island, united by a shared commitment to the memory of April 3rd. TK Taewon Kim Executive Director CK Curie Kim Vice Executive Director JM Jason Moon Secretary TL Tyga Lee Finance Director MK Minjae Kang Marketing Director KK Kyudo Kim Fundraising Director JK Junyoung Kim Human Resources Director"},{"d":"about_ko","p":null,"t":"우리의 목적 기억은우리의 책임입니다. 카멜리아 키퍼스는 제주도의 학생과 주민으로 구성된 공동체로, 4·3사건의 올바른 추모와 인식을 위해 헌신합니다 — 제주를 빚고, 수천 명의 목소리를 앗아갔으며, 결코 잊을 수 없는 한국 역사의 한 장. 1948–1954 사건 기간 25–35k 추정 희생자 76+ 4·3 이후 경과 2025 중학교 교육과정 포함 연도 4·3 저희 소개 소개카멜리아 키퍼스 4·3사건이 올바르게 배워지고, 기억되며, 결코 각주로 축소되지 않도록 헌신하는 제주의 학생과 주민 공동체입니다. I. 제주의 동백꽃 동백(camellia japonica)은 제주의 가장 추운 겨울 내내 피어납니다. 대부분의 꽃과 달리, 꽃잎이 하나씩 흩어지지 않고 꽃 전체가 한 번에 통째로 가지에서 떨어집니다. 이 특성이 4·3사건 이후 동백꽃에 특별한 의미를 부여했습니다. 꽃 전체가 한 번에 떨어지는 모습은 1948년부터 1954년까지의 학살 중 예고 없이 희생된 수천 명의 생명을 상징하게 되었습니다."},{"d":"about_ko","p":null,"t":"꽃 전체가 한 번에 떨어지는 모습은 1948년부터 1954년까지의 학살 중 예고 없이 희생된 수천 명의 생명을 상징하게 되었습니다. 오늘날 붉은 동백 배지는 제주의 매년 추모식에서 착용되며, 동백꽃은 4·3사건의 공식 상징으로 자리잡았습니다. 카멜리아 키퍼스는 이 상징과, 그것이 담은 기억을 보존해야 할 책임에서 이름을 따왔습니다. 동백은 통째로 떨어진다 — 그들도 그러했다. 붉은 동백은 매년 4월 3일, 제주 4·3 평화공원에서 희생자를 추모하는 이들이 달고 옵니다. 동백꽃은 기억의 행위 자체와 불가분의 관계가 되었습니다. II. 4·3사건 제주 4·3사건은 의지에 반해 분단된 나라의 정치적 균열에서 비롯된, 비인간화와 박해, 억압의 비극적 역사입니다. 제주도, 1948–1954 — 사건의 시기 1945 — 한국의 분단 제2차 세계대전이 끝나면서, 한반도는 38선을 따라 남북으로 분단되는 전례 없는 위기를 맞았습니다 — 북쪽은 소련, 남쪽은 미국의 점령 하에 놓였습니다. 영구 분단의 가능성은 전 국민에게 깊고 해결하기 어려운 긴장을 불러일으켰습니다."},{"d":"about_ko","p":null,"t":"영구 분단의 가능성은 전 국민에게 깊고 해결하기 어려운 긴장을 불러일으켰습니다. 1948 — 단독 선거 남한의 우익 정당들은 유엔 한국임시위원단(UNTOK)의 승인 아래 1948년 5월 10일 단독 총선을 선언했습니다. 많은 이들이 이 선거가 통일의 희망을 영구히 끝낼 것을 두려워했습니다. 제주도에서는 이러한 긴장이 특히 극심했습니다. 1948년 4월 3일 — 봉기 계획된 선거에 반대하여, 제주의 좌익 세력은 1948년 4월 3일 경찰서와 우익 인사들을 향해 봉기했습니다. 정치적 저항으로 시작된 것이 장기간의 국가 주도 토벌 작전으로 발전하여 대규모 민간인 학살로 이어졌습니다. 1948–1954 — 학살 갈등은 6년간 지속되었습니다. 제주 인구의 상당한 비율에 해당하는 2만 5천에서 3만 5천 명이 폭력적인 탄압으로 사망했습니다. 마을이 불에 타고, 가족이 파괴되었으며, 생존자들은 평생의 트라우마에 시달렸습니다. 만행의 전체 규모는 수십 년간 의도적으로 은폐되었습니다."},{"d":"about_ko","p":null,"t":"만행의 전체 규모는 수십 년간 의도적으로 은폐되었습니다. 1948년부터 1954년까지 국가 주도의 탄압으로 2만 5천에서 3만 5천 명 — 제주 전체 인구의 상당한 비율 — 이 희생되었습니다. III. 침묵에서 기억으로 학살 이후 수십 년간, 4·3사건에 관한 정보는 억압되었습니다. 생존자와 그 가족들은 침묵을 강요당했습니다. 사건의 전모가 널리 알려지고 인정받기 시작한 것은 비교적 최근의 일입니다. 오늘날 한국에서 4·3사건을 전혀 모르는 사람은 많지 않습니다 — 그러나 인식이 퍼진 것이 광범위한 오해를 바로잡지는 못했습니다. 스스로 연구하지 않는 한 상당수가 여전히 부정확한 이해를 갖고 있습니다. 고등학교 교육과정에서 4·3을 다루기 시작한 것은 2010년대 초반이었으며, 중학교 교육과정에는 2025년에 추가되었습니다. IV. 우리의 책임 제주도에서 생활하고 공부하는 학생과 주민으로서, 저희는 4·3사건을 올바르게 배우고 추모하는 책임에 직면하는 것이 중요하다고 믿습니다. 이것이 저희의 주된 목표입니다."},{"d":"about_ko","p":null,"t":"이것이 저희의 주된 목표입니다. 희생자들이 겪은 피해를 온전히 전달하는 것은 불가능할 수 있지만, 저희는 더 많은 이들에게 인식을 높이고 사건을 알리는 것 역시 그만큼 가치 있다고 생각합니다. 카멜리아 키퍼스는 글, 공동체, 그리고 기억에 대한 변함없는 헌신을 통해 4·3사건의 올바른 인식과 이해를 강화하는 것을 목표로 합니다. V. 이사회 카멜리아 키퍼스는 4·3 기억에 대한 공동의 헌신으로 뭉친 제주도의 학생과 주민들이 이끌고 있습니다. TK Taewon Kim 총장 CK Curie Kim 부총장 JM Jason Moon 총무 TL Tyga Lee 재무부장 MK Minjae Kang 마케팅부장 KK Kyudo Kim 모금부장 JK Junyoung Kim 인사부장"}],"vectors":"","scales":""};
